"Capsicum annuum","count":1},{"key":"cid:54670067","title":"Vitamin C","count":1},{"key":"cid:62640","title":"Zinc Sulfate Heptahydrate","count":1},{"key":"cid:154911","title":"Calcium D-glucarate","count":1}]},"cid:19371515":{"key":"cid:19371515","title":"Alcaftadine","compound":{"key":"cid:19371515","cid":19371515,"qid":"Q4712900","title":"Alcaftadine","iupac":"11-(1-methylpiperidin-4-ylidene)-5,6-dihydroimidazo[2,1-b][3]benzazepine-3-carbaldehyde","wiki_url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alcaftadine","cas":"147084-10-4","iuphar":"7587","drugbank":"DB06766","chemspider":"14201635","unii":"7Z8O94ECSX","kegg":"D06552","chebi":"71023","chembl":"CHEMBL1201747","pdb_ligand":null,"comptox":"DTXSID80598455","echa":null,"description":"chemical compound","kind":null,"atc":"S01GX11","mesh":"C557708","uniprot":null,"ncbi_taxon":null,"gbif":null,"powo":null,"extra_at":"2026-11-08T19:46:30+00:00","eml":null,"smiles":"CN1CCC(=C2C3=CC=CC=C3CCN4C2=NC=C4C=O)CC1","chembl_phase":4.0,"first_approval":2010,"bbw":0,"fda":1,"ema":0,"control":null,"reg_at":"2026-11-10T10:45:10+00:00"},"status":null,"pharm":[{"section":"Drug Indication","items":[{"section":"Drug Indication","text":"For the prevention of itching associated with allergic conjunctivitis.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB06766","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"},{"section":"Drug Indication","text":"FDA Label","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB06766","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"MeSH Pharmacological Classification","items":[{"section":"MeSH Pharmacological Classification","text":"Drugs that selectively bind to but do not activate histamine H1 receptors, thereby blocking the actions of endogenous histamine. Included here are the classical antihistaminics that antagonize or prevent the action of histamine mainly in immediate hypersensitivity. They act in the bronchi, capillaries, and some other smooth muscles, and are used to prevent or allay motion sickness, seasonal rhinitis, and allergic dermatitis and to induce somnolence. The effects of blocking central nervous system H1 receptors are not as well understood.","source":"Medical Subject Headings (MeSH)","url":"https://id.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/M0010395.html","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/copyright.html"}]},{"section":"Pharmacodynamics","items":[{"section":"Pharmacodynamics","text":"Following bilateral topical ocular administration of alcaftadine ophthalmic solution, 0.25%, the mean plasma Cmax of alcaftadine was approximately 60 pg/mL and the median Tmax occurred at 15 minutes. Plasma concentrations of alcaftadine were below the lower limit of quantification (10 pg/mL) by 3 hours after dosing. The mean Cmax of the active carboxylic acid metabolite was approximately 3 ng/mL and occurred at 1 hour after dosing. Plasma concentrations of the carboxylic acid metabolite were below the lower limit of quantification (100 pg/mL) by 12 hours after dosing.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB06766","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Mechanism of Action","items":[{"section":"Mechanism of Action","text":"Alcaftadine is a H1 histamine receptor antagonist and inhibitor of the release of histamine from mast cells. Decreased chemotaxis and inhibition of eosinophil activation has also been demonstrated.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB06766","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","items":[{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","text":"Based on data following oral administration of alcaftadine, the carboxylic acid metabolite is primarily eliminated unchanged in the urine.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB06766","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Protein Binding","items":[{"section":"Protein Binding","text":"The protein binding of alcaftadine and the active metabolite are 39.2% and 62.7% respectively.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB06766","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Metabolism/Metabolites","items":[{"section":"Metabolism/Metabolites","text":"The metabolism of alcaftadine is mediated by non-CYP450 cytosolic enzymes to the active carboxylic acid metabolite.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB06766","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Biological Half-Life","items":[{"section":"Biological Half-Life","text":"The elimination half-life of the carboxylic acid metabolite is approximately 2 hours following topical ocular administration.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB06766","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]}],"safety":[],"manufacturing":[],"structure":true,"populations":{"noswallow":{"label":"No tablet or capsule to swallow","count":7,"forms":[["Ophthalmic Solution",6],["sterile ophthalmic solution",1]]}},"reg":{"fda":1,"ema":0,"phase":4.0,"first_approval":2010,"bbw":false,"control":null,"withdrawn":[],"reg_checked":true,"aware":null},"chem":{"props":{"key":"cid:19371515","title":"Alcaftadine","products":7,"mw":307.4,"logp":2.78,"tpsa":38.1,"hbd":0,"hba":3,"rotb":1,"rings":4,"heavy":23,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C19H21N3O"},"similar":[{"key":"cid:2913","title":"Cyproheptadine","products":3,"mw":287.4,"logp":4.7,"tpsa":3.2,"hbd":0,"hba":1,"rotb":0,"rings":4,"heavy":22,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C21H21N","similarity":0.408},{"key":"cid:3827","title":"Ketotifen","products":16,"mw":309.4,"logp":4.01,"tpsa":20.3,"hbd":0,"hba":3,"rotb":0,"rings":4,"heavy":22,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C19H19NOS","similarity":0.397},{"key":"cid:3957","title":"Loratadine","products":30,"mw":382.9,"logp":4.89,"tpsa":42.4,"hbd":0,"hba":3,"rotb":1,"rings":4,"heavy":27,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C22H23ClN2O2","similarity":0.292},{"key":"cid:133017","title":"Rupatadine","products":17,"mw":416.0,"logp":5.63,"tpsa":29.0,"hbd":0,"hba":3,"rotb":2,"rings":5,"heavy":30,"lipinski":1,"formula":"C26H26ClN3","similarity":0.28},{"key":"cid:6451149","title":"Netupitant","products":2,"mw":578.6,"logp":6.79,"tpsa":39.7,"hbd":0,"hba":4,"rotb":5,"rings":4,"heavy":41,"lipinski":2,"formula":"C30H32F6N4O","similarity":0.256},{"key":"cid:2160","title":"Amitriptyline","products":3,"mw":277.4,"logp":4.17,"tpsa":3.2,"hbd":0,"hba":1,"rotb":3,"rings":3,"heavy":21,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C20H23N","similarity":0.246},{"key":"cid:4205","title":"Mirtazapine","products":12,"mw":265.4,"logp":2.48,"tpsa":19.4,"hbd":0,"hba":3,"rotb":0,"rings":4,"heavy":20,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C17H19N3","similarity":0.231},{"key":"cid:4450","title":"Nefopam","products":2,"mw":253.3,"logp":3.24,"tpsa":12.5,"hbd":0,"hba":2,"rotb":1,"rings":3,"heavy":19,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C17H19NO","similarity":0.23}]},"eml":false,"names":[["Alcaftadine",7]],"atc":[{"code":"S01GX11","path":[{"code":"S","label":"Sensory organs"},{"code":"S01","label":"Ophthalmologicals"},{"code":"S01G","label":null},{"code":"S01GX","label":null},{"code":"S01GX11","label":null}]}],"by_type":{"human":7},"co_ingredients":[]},"cid:164522":{"key":"cid:164522","title":"Bepotastine","compound":{"key":"cid:164522","cid":164522,"qid":"Q4890915","title":"Bepotastine","iupac":"4-[4-[(S)-(4-chlorophenyl)-pyridin-2-ylmethoxy]piperidin-1-yl]butanoic acid","wiki_url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bepotastine","cas":"125602-71-3","iuphar":"7466","drugbank":null,"chemspider":"144228","unii":"HYD2U48IAS","kegg":"D09705","chebi":"71204","chembl":"CHEMBL1201758","pdb_ligand":null,"comptox":"DTXSID90904947","echa":null,"description":"chemical compound","kind":null,"atc":null,"mesh":null,"uniprot":null,"ncbi_taxon":null,"gbif":null,"powo":null,"extra_at":"2026-11-08T19:46:36+00:00","eml":null,"smiles":"C1CN(CCC1O[C@@H](C2=CC=C(C=C2)Cl)C3=CC=CC=N3)CCCC(=O)O","chembl_phase":4.0,"first_approval":2009,"bbw":0,"fda":0,"ema":0,"control":null,"reg_at":"2026-11-10T10:47:12+00:00"},"status":null,"pharm":[{"section":"Drug Indication","items":[{"section":"Drug Indication","text":"For the symptomatic treatment of itchy eyes (caused by IgE-induced mast cell degranulation) due to allergic conjunctivitis.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB04890","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"},{"section":"Drug Indication","text":"FDA Label","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB04890","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Drug Classes","items":[{"section":"Drug Classes","text":"Breast Feeding; Lactation; Milk, Human; Anti-Allergic Agents; Antihistamines","source":"Drugs and Lactation Database (LactMed)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/n/lactmed/LM791/","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/copyright.html"}]},{"section":"Pharmacodynamics","items":[{"section":"Pharmacodynamics","text":"Bepotastine is a non-sedating, selective antagonist of the histamine 1 (H1) receptor. It belongs to the second-generation piperidine chemical class. It is a mast cell stabilizer and suppresses the migration of eosinophils into inflamed tissues. Furthermore, bepotastine does not interact with serotonin, muscarinic, benzodiazepine, and beta-adrenergic receptor that would otherwise result in adverse reactions such as dry mouth or sonmolence.   Onset of action = 0.25 hours;  Duration of action = 12-24 hours;","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB04890","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Mechanism of Action","items":[{"section":"Mechanism of Action","text":"Because of a type 1 hypersensitivity reaction cascade that is triggered by antigen exposure, allergic conjunctivitis occurs. Allergen exposure is followed by conjunctival mast cell degranulation and histamine released as a result of the formation of complementary IgE cross-links on the conjunctiva. Due to the release of histamine, symptoms such as itching can be observed. Bepotastine works to relieve itchy eyes by three primary mechanisms of action. It is a non-sedating, selective antagonist of the histamine 1 (H1) receptor, a mast cell stabilizer, and it suppresses the migration of eosinophils into inflamed tissues to prevent tissue damage and worsening of allergic inflammation of the conjunctiva.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB04890","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","items":[{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","text":"Tmax, after single dose, opthalmic = 1.2 hours; Cmax, 1.5%, opthalmic dose = 7.3 ±1.9  ng/mL; After 24 hours post-installation, levels of bepotastine are below quantifiable limit of 2 ng/mL. Minimal systemic absorption with opthalmic dosage form.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB04890","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"},{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","text":"When a oral dose of 2.5 - 40 mg bepotastine is given, 75%-90% of the dose was excreted unchanged in the urine by 24 hours.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB04890","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Protein Binding","items":[{"section":"Protein Binding","text":"55.4% mean plasma protein binding with 10 mg oral dose. Extent of protein binding is independent of plasma drug concentration.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB04890","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Metabolism/Metabolites","items":[{"section":"Metabolism/Metabolites","text":"Minimal metabolism via CYP enzymes","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB04890","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"},{"section":"Metabolism/Metabolites","text":"Minimal metabolism via CYP enzymes \nRoute of Elimination: When a oral dose of 2.5 - 40 mg bepotastine is given, 75%-90% of the dose was excreted unchanged in the urine by 24 hours. \nHalf Life: Elimination half life = 2.5 hours","source":"Toxin and Toxin Target Database (T3DB)","url":"http://www.t3db.ca/toxins/T3D3066","license_url":"https://t3db.ca/downloads"}]},{"section":"Biological Half-Life","items":[{"section":"Biological Half-Life","text":"Elimination half life = 2.5 hours","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB04890","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]}],"safety":[{"section":"Effects During Pregnancy and Lactation","items":[{"section":"Effects During Pregnancy and Lactation","text":"◉ Summary of Use during Lactation There are no reports of infants breastfed during maternal therapy with bepotastine. Because absorption from the eye is limited, bepotastine would not be expected to cause any adverse effects in breastfed infants. To substantially diminish the amount of drug that reaches the breastmilk after using eye drops, place pressure over the tear duct by the corner of the eye for 1 minute or more, then remove the excess solution with an absorbent tissue. ◉ Effects in Breastfed Infants Relevant published information on bepotastine was not found as of the revision date. In one telephone follow-up study, mothers reported irritability and colicky symptoms in 10% of infants exposed to various antihistamines and drowsiness was reported in 1.6% of infants. None of the reactions required medical attention and none of the patients were using bepotastine. ◉ Effects on Lactation and Breastmilk Antihistamines in relatively high doses given by injection can decrease basal serum prolactin in nonlactating women and in early postpartum women. However, suckling-induced prolactin secretion is not affected by antihistamine pretreatment of postpartum mothers. The prolactin level in a mother with established lactation may not affect her ability to breastfeed. Low ophthalmic doses of bepotastine are unlikely to have the same effect on serum prolactin.","source":"Drugs and Lactation Database (LactMed)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/n/lactmed/LM791/"}]},{"section":"Toxicity Summary","items":[{"section":"Toxicity Summary","text":"Because of a type 1 hypersensitivity reaction cascade that is triggered by antigen exposure, allergic conjunctivitis occurs. Allergen exposure is followed by conjunctival mast cell degranulation and histamine released as a result of the formation of complementary IgE cross-links on the conjunctiva. Due to the release of histamine, symptoms such as itching can be observed. Bepotastine works to relieve itchy eyes by three primary mechanisms of action. It is a non-sedating, selective antagonist of the histamine 1 (H1) receptor, a mast cell stabilizer, and it suppresses the migration of eosinophils into inflamed tissues to prevent tissue damage and worsening of allergic inflammation of the conjunctiva.","source":"Toxin and Toxin Target Database (T3DB)","url":"http://www.t3db.ca/toxins/T3D3066"}]}],"manufacturing":[],"structure":true,"populations":{"noswallow":{"label":"No tablet or capsule to swallow","count":5,"forms":[["Ophthalmic Solution",5]]}},"reg":{"fda":0,"ema":0,"phase":4.0,"first_approval":2009,"bbw":false,"control":null,"withdrawn":[],"reg_checked":true,"aware":null},"chem":{"props":{"key":"cid:164522","title":"Bepotastine","products":5,"mw":388.9,"logp":4.17,"tpsa":62.7,"hbd":1,"hba":4,"rotb":8,"rings":3,"heavy":27,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C21H25ClN2O3"},"similar":[{"key":"cid:3191","title":"Ebastine","products":13,"mw":469.7,"logp":7.22,"tpsa":29.5,"hbd":0,"hba":3,"rotb":9,"rings":4,"heavy":35,"lipinski":1,"formula":"C32H39NO2","similarity":0.485},{"key":"cid:2564","title":"Carbinoxamine","products":6,"mw":290.8,"logp":3.4,"tpsa":25.4,"hbd":0,"hba":3,"rotb":6,"rings":2,"heavy":20,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C16H19ClN2O","similarity":0.406},{"key":"cid:1549000","title":"Levocetirizine","products":29,"mw":388.9,"logp":3.15,"tpsa":53.0,"hbd":1,"hba":4,"rotb":8,"rings":3,"heavy":27,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C21H25ClN2O3","similarity":0.403},{"key":"cid:2678","title":"Cetirizine","products":32,"mw":388.9,"logp":3.15,"tpsa":53.0,"hbd":1,"hba":4,"rotb":8,"rings":3,"heavy":27,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C21H25ClN2O3","similarity":0.403},{"key":"cid:2805","title":"Cloperastine","products":9,"mw":329.9,"logp":4.93,"tpsa":12.5,"hbd":0,"hba":2,"rotb":6,"rings":3,"heavy":23,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C20H24ClNO","similarity":0.381},{"key":"cid:2725","title":"Chlorphenamine","products":106,"mw":274.8,"logp":3.82,"tpsa":16.1,"hbd":0,"hba":2,"rotb":5,"rings":2,"heavy":19,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C16H19ClN2","similarity":0.359},{"key":"cid:33036","title":"Dexchlorpheniramine","products":1,"mw":274.8,"logp":3.82,"tpsa":16.1,"hbd":0,"hba":2,"rotb":5,"rings":2,"heavy":19,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C16H19ClN2","similarity":0.359},{"key":"cid:3658","title":"Hydroxyzine","products":1,"mw":374.9,"logp":3.06,"tpsa":35.9,"hbd":1,"hba":4,"rotb":8,"rings":3,"heavy":26,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C21H27ClN2O2","similarity":0.353}]},"eml":false,"names":[["bepotastine",3],["BEPOTASTINE BESILATE",2]],"atc":[],"by_type":{"human":5},"co_ingredients":[]},"cid:60149":{"key":"cid:60149","title":"Sertindole","compound":{"key":"cid:60149","cid":60149,"qid":"Q418050","title":"Sertindole","iupac":"1-[2-[4-[5-chloro-1-(4-fluorophenyl)indol-3-yl]piperidin-1-yl]ethyl]imidazolidin-2-one","wiki_url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sertindole","cas":"106516-24-9","iuphar":"98","drugbank":"DB06144","chemspider":"54229","unii":"GVV4Z879SP","kegg":"C07567; D00561","chebi":"9122","chembl":"CHEMBL12713","pdb_ligand":null,"comptox":"DTXSID6048967","echa":"100.162.562","description":"chemical compound","kind":null,"atc":"N05AE03","mesh":"C066304","uniprot":null,"ncbi_taxon":null,"gbif":null,"powo":null,"extra_at":"2026-11-08T19:46:36+00:00","eml":null,"smiles":"C1CN(CCC1C2=CN(C3=C2C=C(C=C3)Cl)C4=CC=C(C=C4)F)CCN5CCNC5=O","chembl_phase":4.0,"first_approval":1996,"bbw":0,"fda":0,"ema":1,"control":null,"reg_at":"2026-11-10T10:47:07+00:00"},"status":null,"pharm":[{"section":"Drug Indication","items":[{"section":"Drug Indication","text":"Used in the treatment of schizophrenia.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB06144","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"MeSH Pharmacological Classification","items":[{"section":"MeSH Pharmacological Classification","text":"Agents that control agitated psychotic behavior, alleviate acute psychotic states, reduce psychotic symptoms, and exert a quieting effect. They are used in SCHIZOPHRENIA; senile dementia; transient psychosis following surgery; or MYOCARDIAL INFARCTION; etc. These drugs are often referred to as neuroleptics alluding to the tendency to produce neurological side effects, but not all antipsychotics are likely to produce such effects. Many of these drugs may also be effective against nausea, emesis, and pruritus.","source":"Medical Subject Headings (MeSH)","url":"https://id.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/M0021770.html","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/copyright.html"}]},{"section":"Pharmacodynamics","items":[{"section":"Pharmacodynamics","text":"Sertindole is an atypical antipsychotic at least as effective as haloperidol and risperidone in the treatment of neuroleptic-responsive schizophrenia. Sertindole improves negative symptoms, and is also effective for the treatment of neuroleptic-resistant schizophrenia. Sertindole is generally well tolerated and is associated with a low rate of extrapyramidal symptoms (EPS).","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB06144","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Mechanism of Action","items":[{"section":"Mechanism of Action","text":"Sertindole is an antipsychotic drug with affinity for dopamine D2, serotonin 5-HT2A and 5-HT2C, and alpha1-adrenoreceptors. Preclinical studies suggest that sertindole acts preferentially on limbic and cortical dopaminergic neurons and clinical trials have confirmed that sertindole is effective at a low dopamine D2 occupancy level.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB06144","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","items":[{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","text":"Orally available.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB06144","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Metabolism/Metabolites","items":[{"section":"Metabolism/Metabolites","text":"Hepatic. Sertindole is metabolized by cytochrome P450 isoenzymes CYP 2D6 and CYP 3A4.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB06144","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"},{"section":"Metabolism/Metabolites","text":"Sertindole has known human metabolites that include 5-Chloro-1-(4-fluorophenyl)-3-(piperidin-4-yl)-1H-indole and 1-[2-[4-[5-Chloro-1-(4-fluorophenyl)indol-3-yl]piperidin-1-yl]ethyl]-4-hydroxyimidazolidin-2-one.","source":"NORMAN Suspect List Exchange","url":null,"license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"}]},{"section":"Biological Half-Life","items":[{"section":"Biological Half-Life","text":"3 days","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB06144","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]}],"safety":[],"manufacturing":[],"structure":true,"populations":{},"reg":{"fda":0,"ema":1,"phase":4.0,"first_approval":1996,"bbw":false,"control":null,"withdrawn":[{"chembl":"CHEMBL12713","country":"Germany; European Union; Ireland; United Kingdom; Italy; Netherlands; Bulgaria","year":1998,"class":"cardiotoxicity","description":"Arrhythmias and Sudden Cardiac Death (Arrhythmias and Sudden)","ref_url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/009286150103500134"},{"chembl":"CHEMBL12713","country":"Germany; Europ